                <pb facs="0568" n="456" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg456"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7468: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-30T14:00:00-05:00">November 30, 1935—2
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>64. Nanking’s 114, November 28, 5 p.m., first paragraph. The Chinese Ambassador
                    called yesterday on the Secretary and communicated information with regard to
                    recent developments in the North China situation.</p>
                <p>The substance of the comment made by the Secretary to the Ambassador was that
                    this Government was watching the situation closely <pb facs="0569" n="457"
                        xml:id="pg457"/> and carefully considering the question of the course which
                    it should take.</p>
                <p>Repeat to Tokyo as Department’s No. 187.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
